[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120971"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/a66a57932dbc935b5ed6f74b4f0faa0047c4b7ef58ded46be088dad30fa5eee4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/52efe97fcffa3da05151b3ffef5305e0c3ec0d567d3f62b964a30740c08a3981.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/aa100568c18b31507c775e77ed3201a5aed6f708b541e48046617a43fe9a8304.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-121090"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121272/selection/cb55ba02408aac42ef3670a6720fbf6239d8d735b8b4742837044cfcedd36f46" :answer/id #uuid "c884b4e9-f527-582d-be6d-56943d1f1f97" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/068f75ffaabb14f9c68941066a408d31fc7579ab3881e2d8e4b545044b3cd8a9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121272/selection/514d96ba4b99c4b70f4be4d7eb6dc6d1aea7b6d57f8e109ab275cfe04f8b1659" :answer/id #uuid "e5286a77-c9d6-5a3a-b0a2-93ddf4b6d2c9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/5588d90960db9c441d970ffb9ce68a88c2c207806684af4c74e3b296d8919635.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121272/selection/15e42d84e51f06901ddc15ff03fc8d614c5281c91d717967a8008d1b9bdf9c64" :answer/id #uuid "04199958-5d5e-5ba5-80e2-da514c357032" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/q