:question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/3222e808ccb652a4bfb3b3291099b87f5a1db5d4d38635d58c6649d8efc71f06.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/e8ab67f737067fb0e0bfc64a8060fdefacf79ba430bdf9a24d21dbc7893b1cf7.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/42e3960fe067a02d6263df15ede7224ecd6b977c744139889d83529d873096bf.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-121272/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-149550"] :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/e4d289aef282e076b062aab6d8f0f209396313e2c6403b2e27d1c0272ac90795.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/4cef3532a8e70098c030f2341800f06e9e54e4074126a0a97aecdf1f1c5185d0.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/c9ed172c5746b1b0afd2d9b7b3bbb21245115df164bfb7e34f4f530887376e28.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-326445/field-1/4e1443bd31b83d9674b14c2b39fdc69b7291d3991d33de8027815afac4720949" :answer/id #uuid "d7422b24-5612-5acb-ad4a-265e834503a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "+" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326445/field-1/ccfe516fb6b2e6a04cc571ee2ff57ef9ea3f598a00c4bac0e1422b5f957652bc" :answer/id #uuid "8fc42baf-aff5-5693-8c51-9dea1d7d42c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326445/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf269d6-0165-587b-bd6e-aed9f3adb181" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-326445/field-1/ccfe516fb6b2e6a04cc571ee2ff57ef9ea3f598a00c4bac0e1422b5f957652bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-326445/field-1/4e1443bd31b83d9674b14c2b39fdc69b7291d3991d33de8027815afac4720949" "q-326445/field-1/ccfe516fb6b2e6a04cc571ee2ff57ef9ea3f598a00c4bac0e1422b5f9576